{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And all who went up, etc., after he had buried him, etc.</b> The verse's saying ״all who went up״ is to say that not a single man was missing from the entire great camp — both chariots and horsemen — and it gives the reason: for all of them went for a matter of mitzvah to bury his father, and not one of them turned back until Joseph buried his father. And this is what it says: <b>and he returned</b>, etc., <b>and all who went up</b>, etc. — all of them returned. And the reason for ״after he had buried him״ is that all of them — their going was for a matter of mitzvah, and those sent for a mitzvah suffer no harm neither in their going nor in their returning (Pesaḥim 8a)." "Or it may intend along this line: <b>And Joseph returned, etc., and all who went up</b> — and you shall know by which 'went up' I am multiplying the word 'all' to say that not a single man was missing from them: those whose intention in going up was to bury his father. And by what will these be distinguished from the others? For this it concludes: <b>after he had buried his father</b> — those who lingered until after he had buried him, etc.; their intent was recognized through their actions, that they came for the sake of a mitzvah, and of them not one died. But those who went up for another reason are not included in the matter."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 50, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:51:19.233985357Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}